{"id":"c19db815-45fa-42c2-a927-e428c6c2de79","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885911,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    core = case.get('core_distance', {})\\n    distances = case.get('distances', [])\\n    if not nodes or not core or not distances:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\\n    edges = []\\n    for a, b, d in distances:\\n        w = max(core[a], core[b], d)\\n        u, v = (a, b) if a < b else (b, a)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that computes an MST based on mutual reachability distance. The input case has nodes (list of strings), core_distance (dict mapping node to float), distances (list of [node1, node2, distance]). Compute mutual_reachability = max(core[node1], core[node2], distance). Build edges with that weight. Sort edges by (weight, node1, node2) lexicographically (node strings). Then run Kruskal's algorithm using Union-Find to select n-1 edges. Return {\"mst_edges\": list of [node1, node2, weight], \"total_weight\": sum of weights}. Ensure deterministic tie-breaking: sort by weight then node1 then node2. Use only standard library. No mutation of input. Raise ValueError if graph is disconnected (cannot form MST). Also ensure all required fields present? The spec says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" But no explicit schema for input; we can assume case has nodes, core_distance, distances. If missing, ValueError. Return exactly as per output schema: mst_edges array of arrays with exactly 3 items: string, string, number. total_weight number.\n\nImplementation notes: Use union-find with path compression and union by rank. Sort edges. Iterate, if find(u) != find(v), add edge, union, accumulate weight. After loop, if len(selected) != len(nodes)-1: raise ValueError(\"Disconnected graph\").\n\nMust ensure edge list is sorted by (weight, node1, node2) where node1<node2? The spec says \"Canonicalize undirected endpoints.\" So for each pair, store (min, max) lexicographically. That ensures consistency. Also \"Sort by reachability then endpoint IDs.\" So primary sort by weight, then by node1, then node2.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable. Use float.\n\nWrite solve(case) as described.\n\nNow produce JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a JSON string.\n\nBe careful with escaping: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". The entire response must be a single JSON object. No extra text.\n\nLet's craft the Python code.\n\nWe'll define solve(case):\n  nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\n  core = case.get('core_distance', {})\n  distances = case.get('distances', [])\n  if not nodes or not core or not distances: raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n  # Build all unique edges? distances list might contain each pair once. We'll compute mutual reachability for each.\n  edges = []\n  for a, b, d in distances:\n      w = max(core[a], core[b], d)\n      u, v = (a, b) if a < b else (b, a)  # canonical ordering\n      edges.append((w, u, v))\n  # Sort: primary weight, then u, then v\n  edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\n  # Union-Find\n  parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\n  rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\n  def find(x):\n      while parent[x] != x:\n          parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n          x = parent[x]\n      return x\n  def union(x, y):\n      rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n      if rx == ry: return False\n      if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n          parent[rx] = ry\n      elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n          parent[ry] = rx\n      else:\n          parent[ry] = rx\n          rank[rx] += 1\n      return True\n  mst_edges = []\n  total_weight = 0.0\n  for w, u, v in edges:\n      if union(u, v):\n          mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\n          total_weight += w\n          if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n              break\n  if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\n      raise ValueError(\"Disconnected graph\")\n  return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n\nMake sure no mutations to input; we're reading only.\n\nNow produce JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}. Escape properly.\n\nI'll write the code as a string with proper escaping. Use triple quotes maybe but simpler to write as one line? Better to use explicit \\n.\n\nLet's do it.\n\nNote: The output format: must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final response: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    core = case.get('core_distance', {})\\n    distances = case.get('distances', [])\\n    if not nodes or not core or not distances:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\\n    edges = []\\n    for a, b, d in distances:\\n        w = max(core[a], core[b], d)\\n        u, v = (a, b) if a < b else (b, a)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\\n\"}\n\nCheck escaping: In JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. The code uses single quotes inside, which need to be escaped as \\'. But we can use double quotes for string in Python? Actually we used single quotes for literals like 'Missing required fields' etc. To avoid escaping, we can use double quotes in Python code. That might be simpler. The JSON string will be inside double quotes. So we can use double quotes for all Python strings? But then we need to escape any double quotes inside the Python code. If we use single quotes inside Python, then the only characters to escape are backslashes and newlines. But JSON requires backslashes to be escaped as \\\\. We have backslashes in code? No. However we have \\n for newline. In JSON string, backslash is escape, so we need to write \\\\n to represent a literal backslash followed by n? Actually for newline in source code, we want the string to contain a newline character. In JSON, we represent newline as \\n (two characters: backslash and n). But the JSON parser will convert that to a newline. So we need to write \\\\n in the JSON string? Wait: In JSON, a string can contain escape sequences like \\n. So when we write \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = ...\", the \\n is interpreted as newline. So we just write \\n. But we need to escape backslashes if we want literal backslash. In our code we have no backslashes. So we can put newline as \\n directly. However careful: In a JSON string, backslash is an escape character, so to include a literal backslash we use \\\\. We don't have those. So we can write:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    core = case.get('core_distance', {})\\n    ...\"\n\nBut note: inside the Python code, we use single quotes. That's fine. However the JSON string itself is delimited by double quotes. So we need to ensure that any double quote inside the Python code is escaped. Since we use single quotes, no problem. But we also have dictionary literals with curly braces, no issue.\n\nBut we used parentheses in function definitions; that's fine.\n\nOne more: In the code, we used 'Missing required fields' with single quotes - that's fine.\n\nThus we can produce the JSON string with newline escape sequences.\n\nI'll write the entire code as a single string with \\n for line breaks. It will be readable.\n\nBut note: The JSON must be a valid JSON object. The string will be quite long but that's okay.\n\nLet's produce the final answer.\n\nMake sure there's no trailing whitespace or extra characters.\n\nThe output should be exactly: {\"implementation\":\"... code ...\"}.\n\nI'll write it.\n\nBut also note: The spec says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". So we raise ValueError if missing fields or disconnected.\n\nAlso ensure that we don't modify case. We don't.\n\nNow produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"p